   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to adult care facility\nfinancial statements",
    "basePrintNo": "S10090",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10090-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §461-e, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10090\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to adult care\nfacility financial statements\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo allow adult care facilities (\"ACFs\") to obtain a financial statement\nreview from a Certified Public Accountant (\"CPA\"), instead of an opinion\nor full audit.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the closing paragraph of paragraph (a) of subdivision 4\nof section 461-e of the social services law to allow ACFs to obtain a\nCPA review, instead of an opinion, of financial statements required to\nbe filed with the Department of Health (\"DOH\"). DOH would still be able\nto require an opinion if it determines that it is required and neces-\nsary.\n\nSection 2 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nACFs\" are required by statute to file an annual financial statement with\nDOH that must be accompanied by an opinion from a CPA.\n\nUntil 2019, DOH was accepting CPA reviews of a facility's financial\nreport without requiring a full audit. In 2019, DOH modified and stream-\nlined the ACF Financial Report form in consultation with stakeholders.\nHowever, and in parallel, DOE also changed the CPA certification form\nwithout input from ACFs.\n\nAs a result of the changes made to the. CPA certification form, account-\nants started requiring facilities to commission a full financial audit\nin order to sign off on the report, resulting in substantial, added\ncosts for ACFs - the price of an audit is typically $10,000-$15,000 per\nfacility,\n\nThis legislation would allow facilities to seek a CPA review instead of\nan opinion, consistent with DOH's previous practice, removing the need\nfor a CPA full audit. DOH would still be able to require an opinion if\nnecessary to determine the accuracy of the annual financial statement,\nor for other reasons, if appropriate. This would ease a significant\nburden on providers already facing inflation and staffing shortages.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
